//# The `From` interface: a value of one type made from a value of another.
//# A `?` applies it when the error it propagates is not the function's
//# declared error type. `TryFrom` makes one when the value allows it.

import option.Option

/// A type made from an `E`: `ApiError.from(e)`. A `?` on a `Result<T, E>`
/// in a function that returns `Result<_, F>` applies `F`'s `From<E>` impl
/// to the error it propagates, so a domain error type that wraps the errors
/// of the libraries it calls writes one impl per wrapped type, and each `?`
/// converts:
///
/// ```nomi
/// enum ApiError
///     Database sql.Error
///     NotFound String
/// end
///
/// impl From<sql.Error> for ApiError
///     fn from(e: sql.Error) -> ApiError
///         ApiError.Database(e)
///     end
/// end
///
/// fn count(conn: Conn) -> Result<Int, ApiError>
///     n = db.count(conn)?
///     Ok(n)
/// end
/// ```
///
/// The impl sits on the target type. `E` is an input, so a module may
/// write `impl From<MyError> for TheirError` as well as `impl
/// From<TheirError> for MyError`. A `?` applies one impl, never a chain of
/// them, and a type needs no impl to convert to itself.
pub interface From<E>
    fn from(e: E) -> self
end

/// A type made from an `E` when the `E` allows it: `Some` of the value, or
/// `None`. A `type` with a rule in its chain implements `TryFrom` of its
/// base because of its rule: with `type Percent Int when fn n -> n >= 0
/// and n <= 100`, `Percent.try_from(42)` checks the base's rules and then
/// its own and is `Some(Percent(42))`, and `Percent.try_from(142)` is
/// `None`. The compiler supplies that impl, so writing `impl TryFrom<Int>
/// for Percent` is an error. Impls from other types are written as `From`'s
/// are, one per source type:
///
/// ```nomi
/// type Celsius Float when fn c -> c >= -273.15
///
/// type Kelvin Float when fn k -> k >= 0.0
///
/// impl TryFrom<Celsius> for Kelvin
///     fn try_from(Celsius(c)) -> Option<Kelvin>
///         Kelvin.try_from(c + 273.15)
///     end
/// end
/// ```
///
/// A generic function bounded by `TryFrom<E>` checks a value into any type
/// that has one, `where T: TryFrom<Int>` and then `T.try_from(n)`.
pub interface TryFrom<E>
    fn try_from(value: E) -> Option<self>
end
